timepart_lookup_perf
====================

Measures single-row lookup latency against `classifications` in three states,
using the same query and the same keys throughout:

    SELECT security_identifier, classification_value
      FROM classifications WHERE security_identifier = ?

  arm 1  plain            ordinary table, 200k rows
  arm 2  partition        same table converted to a TIME partition, RETENTION 2
  arm 3  partition_nodoh  same partition, dohsql_disable=1 on every node

`security_identifier` carries a unique key, so every arm is a unique-index
equality seek returning exactly one row.

Why the partition is slower
---------------------------
A time partition is keyed on insert time, not on `security_identifier`, so a
lookup by identifier cannot be routed to one shard -- it has to probe the
unique index of *every* shard and union the results.  With RETENTION 2 that is
two index probes and a union instead of one probe.

dohsql (distributed/parallel sql, "doh") executes those shard probes in
parallel worker threads.  For a large scan that is a win; for a sub-millisecond
point lookup the per-query thread handoff can easily cost more than the second
index probe it is hiding.  Arm 3 disables it so the shards are probed serially
on the sql thread, which separates "cost of being a partition" from "cost of
the parallel machinery".

Partitioning is done with

    ALTER TABLE classifications PARTITIONED BY TIME
        PERIOD 'daily' RETENTION 2 START <tomorrow> RETROACTIVELY

`RETROACTIVELY` matters: it materialises both shards at once, so the union is
real.  Without it there is a single shard until the first rollout, no union,
and arm 3 would measure nothing.  `START` is a day out so no rollout fires
mid-test -- with RETENTION 2 the first rollout drops the oldest shard, which
would take the loaded rows with it.  Because every row is inserted inside a
single period, one shard ends up holding all 200k rows and the other is empty;
the test prints the per-shard counts so this is visible in the output.  The
lookup still probes both.

Running it
----------
    cd tests && make timepart_lookup_perf

Knobs (environment variables):

    ROWS       rows loaded             default 200000
    NLOOKUPS   timed lookups per arm   default 2000
    WARMUP     untimed lookups first   default 200
    REPEATS    timed runs per arm      default 3
    CHUNK      rows per load txn       default 20000

Quick smoke run:

    ROWS=20000 NLOOKUPS=200 REPEATS=1 make timepart_lookup_perf

Reading the output
------------------
Each arm emits `METRIC` lines (one per repeat) with avg/min/p50/p95/p99/max in
microseconds plus qps.  The comparison table at the end uses the *fastest*
repeat of each arm -- on a shared host the fastest run is the least polluted by
co-tenants; averaging repeats would just fold the noise in.

This is a report-only test.  It fails on load errors, query errors, or rows
lost during partitioning -- never on a latency regression, which is not
trustworthy enough to gate on.

Watch the spread, not just the average, on the `partition` arm.  A single-node
run at the defaults looked like this:

    metric              plain        partition      partition_nodoh
    avg_us              252.7           1191.3                341.5
    p50_us                250              470                  312
    p95_us                342            10809                  480
    p99_us                490            11268                  637

The doh arm is bimodal, and the gap to arm 3 has two independent causes that
are worth keeping apart.

The tail -- p95/p99 parked just past 10ms -- is the hardcoded
`poll(NULL, 0, 10)` in `dohsql_end_distribute` (db/dohsql.c).  Once the lookup
itself is done, the coordinator waits for each child shard to reach
DOH_CLIENT_DONE on a flat 10ms sleep rather than a condvar; `_mark_shard_done`
sets that status under the connector mutex and signals nothing.  Rebuilding
with the constant at 3ms moves p95 from 10809us to 3856us and p99 from 11268us
to 4217us, while the plain and nodoh arms do not budge -- the cliff tracks the
constant.  Restoring only the coordinator poll to 10ms (leaving the child-side
poll in `dohsql_wait_for_master` at 3ms) puts the cliff straight back at
10774us, so the coordinator loop is solely responsible.

The median is a different story, and the sleep does not explain it.  Only about
3.5% of lookups hit the sleep at all, so it accounts for roughly 350us of the
~850us average gap; extrapolating the quantum to zero still leaves doh ~500us
and ~2.5x slower than nodoh.  The `min_us` column makes this plain -- a minimum
is a query that hit no sleep, and there doh is only ~40-65us behind nodoh, yet
by the median it is 150-600us behind and varies widely run to run (doh p50
ranged 442-901us across nine repeats against a steady 311-367us for nodoh).
That profile -- cheap floor, expensive and noisy middle -- is the cost of
running the shard in parallel at all: enqueueing it on the sql thread pool,
waiting for it to get CPU, preparing a second sqlite engine, taking its own
curtran, and handing rows back through the queue.  On a 300us point lookup that
setup costs more than the second index probe it hides, which is why disabling
doh wins here.

So: fixing the poll would flatten the tail dramatically and pull the average
from ~1190us to ~840us, but it would not close the gap to the 340us nodoh arm.
Read avg_us as a blend of the two effects and look at min/p50 versus p95/p99 to
tell them apart.

The driver is `tests/tools/timepart-lookup-perf.c`.  The identifier list is
sampled once, before arm 1, and written to `ids.txt`; every arm replays that
same list in the same order, so the only thing varying between arms is the
state of the database.
